{"tool_name":"clustal2","contents":["galaxy","bioschemas","debian","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"clustal2","home":"http://www.clustal.org/clustal2/","license":"LGPL-3.0","summary":"Multiple sequence alignment program with a command-line interface (Clustal W) and a graphical user interface (Clustal X). The display colours allow conserved features to be highlighted for easy viewing in the alignment. It is available for several platforms, including Windows, Macintosh PowerMac, Linux and Solaris.\nNames occassionally spelled also as Clustal W2, ClustalW2, ClustalW, ClustalX, Clustal2.","addition_date":"2017-04-21T12:12:16Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:35:37.959243Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Desktop application"],"version":["2.0 - 2.1"],"collections":["Bioconda","DebianMed","clustal","galaxyPasteur"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:clustal2","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"clustalw@ucd.ie","name":"Clustal helpdesk","typeEntity":"Division","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"Science Foundation Ireland","typeEntity":"Funding agency","url":"http://www.sfi.ie/"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://www.clustal.org/clustal2/#Documentation"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2044"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Multiple sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0492"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0863"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Clustal 2 (Clustal W, Clustal X)","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"jison","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/22.22.4673","metadata":{"abstract":"The sensitivity of the commonly used progressive multiple sequence alignment method has been greatly improved for the alignment of divergent protein sequences. Firstly, individual weights are assigned to each sequence in a partial alignment in order to downweight near-duplicate sequences and up-weight the most divergent ones. Secondly, amino acid substitution matrices are varied at different alignment stages according to the divergence of the sequences to be aligned. Thirdly, residue-specific gap penalties and locally reduced gap penalties in hydrophilic regions encourage new gaps in potential loop regions rather than regular secondary structure. Fourthly, positions in early alignments where gaps have been opened receive locally reduced gap penalties to encourage the opening up of new gaps at these positions. These modifications are incorporated into a new program, CLUSTAL W which is freely available. \u00a9 1994 Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Gibson T.J."},{"name":"Higgins D.G."},{"name":"Thompson J.D."}],"citationCount":58067,"date":"1994-11-11T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"CLUSTAL W: Improving the sensitivity of progressive multiple sequence alignment through sequence weighting, position-specific gap penalties and weight matrix choice"},"note":"Historical publication on CLUSTAL W. Open access to PDF only.","pmcid":"PMC308517","pmid":"7984417","type":["Primary"],"version":"historical CLUSTAL W"},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/25.24.4876","metadata":{"abstract":"CLUSTAL X is a new windows interface for the widely-used progressive multiple sequence alignment program CLUSTAL W. The new system is easy to use, providing an integrated system for performing multiple sequence and profile alignments and analysing the results. CLUSTAL X displays the sequence alignment in a window on the screen. A versatile sequence colouring scheme allows the user to highlight conserved features in the alignment. Pull-down menus provide all the options required for traditional multiple sequence and profile alignment. New features include: the ability to cut-and-paste sequences to change the order of the alignment, selection of a subset of the sequences to be realigned, and selection of a sub-range of the alignment to be realigned and inserted back into the original alignment. Alignment quality analysis can be performed and low-scoring segments or exceptional residues can be highlighted. Quality analysis and realignment of selected residue ranges provide the user with a powerful tool to improve and refine difficult alignments and to trap errors in input sequences. CLUSTAL X has been compiled on SUN Solaris, IRIX5.3 on Silicon Graphics, Digital UNIX on DECstations, Microsoft Windows (32 bit) for PCs, Linux ELF for x86 PCs, and Macintosh PowerMac.","authors":[{"name":"Gibson T.J."},{"name":"Higgins D.G."},{"name":"Jeanmougin F."},{"name":"Plewniak F."},{"name":"Thompson J.D."}],"citationCount":36457,"date":"1997-12-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"The CLUSTAL X windows interface: Flexible strategies for multiple sequence alignment aided by quality analysis tools"},"note":"Historical publication on CLUSTAL X. Open access.","pmcid":"PMC147148","pmid":"9396791","type":["Primary"],"version":"historical CLUSTAL X"},{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btm404","metadata":{"abstract":"Summary: The Clustal W and Clustal X multiple sequence alignment programs have been completely rewritten in C++. This will facilitate the further development of the alignment algorithms in the future and has allowed proper porting of the programs to the latest versions of Linux, Macintosh and Windows operating systems. \u00a9 The Author 2007. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved.","authors":[{"name":"Blackshields G."},{"name":"Brown N.P."},{"name":"Chenna R."},{"name":"Gibson T.J."},{"name":"Higgins D.G."},{"name":"Larkin M.A."},{"name":"Lopez R."},{"name":"McWilliam H."},{"name":"Mcgettigan P.A."},{"name":"Thompson J.D."},{"name":"Valentin F."},{"name":"Wallace I.M."},{"name":"Wilm A."}],"citationCount":24337,"date":"2007-11-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"Clustal W and Clustal X version 2.0"},"note":"Historical publication on CLUSTAL. Closed access.","pmid":"17846036","type":["Other"],"version":"historical CLUSTAL"},{"doi":"10.1016/0378-1119(88)90330-7","metadata":{"abstract":"An approach for performing multiple alignments of large numbers of amino acid or nucleotide sequences is described. The method is based on first deriving a phylogenetic tree from a matrix of all pairwise sequence similarity scores, obtained using a fast pairwise alignment algorithm. Then the multiple alignment is achieved from a series of pairwise alignments of clusters of sequences, following the order of branching in the tree. The method is sufficiently fast and economical with memory to be easily implemented on a microcomputer, and yet the results obtained are comparable to those from packages requiring mainframe computer facilities. \u00a9 1988.","authors":[{"name":"Higgins D.G."},{"name":"Sharp P.M."}],"citationCount":3024,"date":"1988-12-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Gene","title":"CLUSTAL: a package for performing multiple sequence alignment on a microcomputer"},"note":"Historical publication on CLUSTAL. Closed access.","type":["Other"],"version":"historical CLUSTAL"}],"topic":[{"term":"Phylogeny","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0084"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"Clustal 2 (Clustal W, Clustal X)","home":"https://bio.tools/clustal2","license":"LGPL-3.0","version":"2.0 - 2.1","summary":"Multiple sequence alignment program with a command-line interface (Clustal W) and a graphical user interface (Clustal X). The display colours allow conserved features to be highlighted for easy viewing in the alignment. It is available for several platforms, including Windows, Macintosh PowerMac, Linux and Solaris.\nNames occassionally spelled also as Clustal W2, ClustalW2, ClustalW, ClustalX, Clustal2.","tool_type